//? Title: Denoising Autoencoders: Corrupted Inputs, Clean Targets
//? Subtitle: Exact Gaussian corruption, conditional-mean reconstruction, and matched MNIST fits that retain mild-noise failures
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Recovering a clean image from a noisy observation requires assumptions about which image structures
are plausible. A denoising autoencoder learns this mapping from corrupted inputs paired with their
unchanged clean targets. We derive the squared-error objective, check a four-pixel example and its
complete output gradient, and train the original 784–32–784 network on genuine MNIST. A matched
ordinary autoencoder receives clean inputs during fitting; both select checkpoints on the same
fixed noisy validation task. With three epochs, the denoiser's main held-out MSE is 0.05995,
compared with 0.06217 for the raw noisy input and 0.05371 for the ordinary control. At low noise,
leaving the input alone is substantially better. These failures remain part of the example.
)
\toc

> The method

Let \(X\in[0,1]^d\) be a clean observation. The corruption process samples \(Z\sim\mathcal N(0,I)\)
and forms \(Y=\operatorname{clip}(X+sZ,0,1)\). Here \(s\) is in original unit-pixel coordinates,
not a fraction of a standardized feature's variance. The implementation evaluates the addition
in Float64 with a Float32 coefficient, rounds its result to Float32, then clamps to [0,1].
Clipping is not invertible: values at zero and one may have arisen from many different draws.
The corrupted input is detached augmentation data; the clean target is never replaced by it.

The model uses independent dense weights, a ReLU code and sigmoid reconstruction:

$$
H=\max(YW_e+b_e,0),\qquad R_\theta(Y)=\sigma(HW_d+b_d),\qquad
\mathcal L(\theta)=\mathbb E_{X,Z}\left[\frac1d\|R_\theta(Y)-X\|^2\right].
$$
\alttext(The encoder applies an affine map and ReLU to the corrupted input. The decoder produces sigmoid pixels. Expected squared error compares those pixels with the clean original.)

An ordinary autoencoder instead fits \(R_\theta(X)\) to \(X\). The denoising objective discourages
copying the corruption when the data distribution supplies a better reconstruction rule. It
neither guarantees recovery of every image nor implies an advantage over every ordinary
bottleneck model in a finite training run. At inference, the function receives only the observed
image; a clean target is needed to score a test, not to make its prediction.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/denoising_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(56, 1100);
  d.node("x", {0, 0}, "Clean image X\nPreserved target")
      .node("y", {5, 0}, "Corrupted input Y\nAdd Gaussian noise, clip")
      .node("r", {10, 0}, "Reconstruction R(Y)\nReLU code, sigmoid pixels")
      .node("l", {5, -3}, "Mean squared error\nCompare R(Y) with clean X")
      .edge("x", "y")
      .edge("y", "r")
      .edge("x", "l", "Clean target")
      .edge("r", "l", "Prediction")
      .text({5, 2}, "Inference follows Y → R(Y), with no clean target")
      .save_svg("build/denoising_method.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
\image(figures/denoising_method.svg)
// result_end

\caption(The target branch bypasses corruption. Fresh noise changes training inputs, while the original images remain the targets.)
\alttext(A clean image branches into a corruption path leading to a reconstruction and a direct target path leading to the reconstruction loss.)

For \(N\) rows and \(d\) pixels, define
\(D=2(\hat X-X)\odot\hat X\odot(1-\hat X)/(Nd)\), and
\(E=(DW_d^\top)\odot\mathbf1\{YW_e+b_e>0\}\). Then

$$
\nabla_{W_d}L=H^\top D,\quad\nabla_{b_d}L=\sum_i D_i,\qquad
\nabla_{W_e}L=Y^\top E,\quad\nabla_{b_e}L=\sum_i E_i.
$$
\alttext(Decoder gradients use the codes and clean-target residuals. Encoder weight gradients multiply the same backpropagated residuals by the corrupted inputs, not the clean targets.)

Only the encoder's input factor changes from the ordinary autoencoder chain rule. The residual
always uses the clean target. ReLU's derivative at zero is defined as zero. No derivative of the
augmentation is needed to fit these parameters. With a code of width \(k\), forward and reverse
passes require \(O(Ndk)\) arithmetic and \(O(dk+N(d+k))\) parameter/activation storage.
Fresh augmentation is a Monte Carlo approximation to the expected objective. Nonconvex fitting
and a short optimization budget need not approach its population optimum.

> Four pixels: the wrong target would make the loss vanish

Take \(X=((0,0.2),(0.8,1))\), \(Z=((-2,1),(-1,2))\) and \(s=0.3\).
Before clipping, the approximate values are \((-0.6,0.5,0.5,1.6)\); afterwards they are
\((0,0.5,0.5,1)\). If a prediction simply copies this noisy image, its MSE against the clean
original is 0.045, while its MSE against the corrupted input is zero. The four clean-target
output derivatives are \((0,0.15,-0.15,0)\). This is a target check, not a trained network.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/denoising_toy.png", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n      = dm::nn;
  const auto clean = n::tensor({0., .2, .8, 1.}, {2, 2});
  const auto z     = n::tensor({-2., 1., -1., 2.}, {1, 2, 2}, n::DType::Float64);
  const auto noisy = n::models::CorruptForDenoising(clean, z, .3f);
  if (noisy.requires_grad() ||
      n::max(n::abs(n::models::CorruptForDenoising(clean, z, 0) - clean)).item() != 0)
    return 1;
  const auto prediction = noisy.clone().requires_grad_();
  const auto loss       = n::mse(prediction, clean);
  const auto gradient   = n::grad(loss, {prediction})[0];
  std::print(
      "| pixel | clean target | corrupted observation | clean-target derivative "
      "|\n|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n");
  const auto x = clean.to_vector<float>(), y = noisy.to_vector<float>(),
             g = gradient.to_vector<float>();
  for (int i = 0; i < 4; ++i) {
    if (std::abs(g[i] - (double(y[i]) - x[i]) / 2) > 1e-8)
      return 1;
    std::print("| {} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} |\n", i, x[i], y[i], g[i]);
  }
  if (std::abs(loss.item() - .045) > 2e-8)
    return 1;
  std::print(
      "\nClean-target MSE {:.9f}; noisy-target MSE {:.9f}.\n",
      loss.item(),
      n::mse(prediction, noisy).item());
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  n::save_image_grid(
      n::stack({clean, noisy, n::abs(noisy - clean)}, 0).reshape({3, 2, 2, 1}),
      "build/denoising_toy.png",
      3,
      0,
      1,
      2,
      .8,
      64);
}
```
