\caption(Clean image, corrupted image and absolute error on a fixed grayscale [0,1] scale, with enlargement 64. Clipping hides the size of the two out-of-range draws.)
\alttext(Three two by two panels compare clean pixels, noise-corrupted pixels and their absolute differences.)

> Checking the theory: ambiguity produces averages

For an observed image \(Y=y\), write \(m(y)=\mathbb E[X\mid Y=y]\). Expanding a square and using
\(\mathbb E[X-m(y)\mid Y=y]=0\) gives

$$
\mathbb E[\|X-r\|^2\mid Y=y]
=\mathbb E[\|X-m(y)\|^2\mid Y=y]+\|r-m(y)\|^2.
$$
\alttext(Conditional squared error equals conditional variance plus squared distance from the conditional mean.)

Thus an unrestricted squared-error predictor chooses the conditional mean. This is a population
statement; a finite bottleneck fit may not reach it. It explains why averaging several plausible
clean images can produce blur. It is not a statement that the function inverts clipping or
recovers an unknown original exactly.

In a deliberately ambiguous two-observation example, clean scalar pixels 0 and 1 both arrive as
0.5 after corruption. Every deterministic predictor must return the same value \(r\) for both.
Its empirical risk is \(\{r^2+(r-1)^2\}/2=(r-0.5)^2+0.25\). The optimum is grey, not either
original black or white. Six explicit Float64 SGD updates fit this single shared prediction.
The same program checks the seeded Gaussian bank and shows clipping bias near the pixel bounds.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/denoising_conditional_risk.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  n::ParameterStore  p(1, n::DType::Float64);
  const auto         prediction = p.get("shared_pixel", {}, n::init::constant(.1));
  const auto         target     = n::tensor({0., 1.}, {2, 1}, n::DType::Float64);
  n::OptimizerConfig config;
  config.kind = n::OptimizerKind::Sgd;
  n::Optimizer optimizer(p, .25, config);
  for (int step = 0; step < 6; ++step) {
    const auto output = n::broadcast_to(prediction, {2, 1});
    optimizer.minimize(n::mse(output, target));
  }
  const double fitted = prediction.item();
  const double risk   = n::mse(n::broadcast_to(prediction, {2, 1}), target).item();
  if (std::abs(fitted - .49375) > 1e-13 || std::abs(risk - .2500390625) > 1e-13)
    return 1;
  std::print(
      "Six SGD updates: prediction {:.8f}; risk {:.10f}; optimal risk 0.25.\n\n", fitted, risk);
  const auto bank = n::models::GaussianNoise({100000, 1}, 1, 5489);
  if (bank.to_vector<double>() !=
      n::models::GaussianNoise({100000, 1}, 1, 5489).to_vector<double>())
    return 1;
  const double mean     = n::mean(bank).item();
  const double variance = n::mean(n::square(bank)).item() - mean * mean;
  if (std::abs(mean) > .015 || std::abs(variance - 1) > .02)
    return 1;
  std::print("100,000 standard draws: mean {:.6f}; variance {:.6f}.\n\n", mean, variance);
  std::print("| clean constant pixel | mean corrupted pixel, sigma 0.35 |\n|---:|---:|\n");
  for (double value : {0., .5, 1.})
    std::print(
        "| {:.1f} | {:.6f} |\n",
        value,
        n::mean(n::models::CorruptForDenoising(n::full({100000, 1}, value), bank, .35f)).item());
  std::vector<double> values, risks;
  for (int i = 0; i <= 100; ++i) {
    const double r = double(i) / 100;
    values.push_back(r);
    risks.push_back((r - .5) * (r - .5) + .25);
  }
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.lines(values, risks, "Exact ambiguous-pixel risk", {37, 99, 235}, 2.)
      .points({fitted}, {risk}, "After six SGD updates", {220, 38, 38})
      .x_label("shared reconstruction value r")
      .y_label("mean squared error")
      .title("Two clean pixels, one indistinguishable observation")
      .save_svg("build/denoising_conditional_risk.svg");
}
```
