| denoising | decoder/b | 0.00732623 |
| ordinary | encoder/W | 0.00779895 |
| ordinary | encoder/b | 0.00128825 |
| ordinary | decoder/W | 0.01079268 |
| ordinary | decoder/b | 0.00732713 |
\image(figures/denoising_learning.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Both post-epoch curves score the same fixed corrupted validation images against clean targets. The ordinary model trains on clean inputs but validates on this noisy task.)
\alttext(Two validation loss curves over three epochs compare noise-trained and ordinary autoencoders on the same denoising objective.)

> Held-out amplitudes, an independent noise draw and deployment checks

The selected parameters are restored into fresh stores. Both functions receive only an input
image during prediction. Exact encode/decode and repeated-call comparisons, single-versus-batch
parity and immutable stored weights are checked. The fitting pixel mean is a constant baseline
estimated using fitting pixels only. For each input condition, all predictors are scored against
the same clean held-out images. Test results never change the selected checkpoints.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  std::map<std::string, n::Tensor> a;
  for (const auto& [name, value] : n::load_npz("build/denoising_data.npz"))
    a.emplace(name, value);
  const auto clean = a.at("test_images").reshape({200, 784});
  const auto mean  = n::broadcast_to(
      n::cast(
          n::mean(n::cast(a.at("fit_images").reshape({1000, 784}), n::DType::Float64), {0}, true),
          n::DType::Float32),
      clean.shape());
  n::ParameterStore denoising(1657), ordinary(1657);
  denoising.load("build/denoising_denoising_best.npz", false);
  ordinary.load("build/denoising_ordinary_best.npz", false);
  const auto      before = denoising.flatten(), ordinary_before = ordinary.flatten();
  n::NoGradGuard  guard;
  n::NamedTensors outputs{{"mean", mean}};
  std::print(
      "| input case | raw input MSE | noise-trained MSE | ordinary AE MSE "
      "|\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n");
  for (const auto& [name, key] : std::vector<std::pair<std::string, std::string>>{
           {"clean", "test_images"},
           {"low", "test_low"},
           {"noisy", "test_corrupted"},
           {"second", "test_second"},
           {"strong", "test_strong"}}) {
    const auto   input = a.at(key).reshape({200, 784});
    const auto   d     = n::models::UndercompleteAutoencoder(denoising, input);
    const auto   o     = n::models::UndercompleteAutoencoder(ordinary, input);
    const double raw =
        n::mse(n::cast(input, n::DType::Float64), n::cast(clean, n::DType::Float64)).item();
    const double score =
        n::mse(n::cast(d.reconstruction, n::DType::Float64), n::cast(clean, n::DType::Float64))
            .item();
    const double control =
        n::mse(n::cast(o.reconstruction, n::DType::Float64), n::cast(clean, n::DType::Float64))
            .item();
    std::print("| {} | {:.9f} | {:.9f} | {:.9f} |\n", name, raw, score, control);
    outputs.emplace_back(name + "_denoising_code", d.code);
    outputs.emplace_back(name + "_denoising_reconstruction", d.reconstruction);
    outputs.emplace_back(name + "_ordinary_reconstruction", o.reconstruction);
    const auto repeat  = n::models::UndercompleteAutoencoder(denoising, input);
    const auto single  = n::models::UndercompleteAutoencoder(denoising, n::take_rows(input, {0}));
    const auto decoded = n::dense(denoising, "decoder", d.code, 784, n::Activation::Sigmoid);
    if (n::max(n::abs(repeat.reconstruction - d.reconstruction)).item() != 0 ||
        n::max(n::abs(decoded - d.reconstruction)).item() != 0 ||
        n::max(n::abs(single.reconstruction - n::take_rows(d.reconstruction, {0}))).item() > 1e-6)
      return 1;
  }
  if (before != denoising.flatten() || ordinary_before != ordinary.flatten())
    return 1;
  n::save_npz("build/denoising_outputs.npz", outputs);
  std::print(
      "\nFitting-mean MSE {:.9f}. Fresh restore, target-free inference, exact decode/repeat and "
      "immutable weights pass.\n",
      n::mse(n::cast(mean, n::DType::Float64), n::cast(clean, n::DType::Float64)).item());
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| input case | raw input MSE | noise-trained MSE | ordinary AE MSE |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| clean | 0.000000000 | 0.064962645 | 0.050512425 |
| low | 0.012016544 | 0.061015408 | 0.051245820 |
| noisy | 0.062168205 | 0.059952738 | 0.053709699 |
| second | 0.062143021 | 0.060118123 | 0.053859845 |
| strong | 0.149556864 | 0.062646353 | 0.058082961 |

Fitting-mean MSE 0.067798074. Fresh restore, target-free inference, exact decode/repeat and immutable weights pass.
// result_end

\caption(All MSE values use clean targets. The second-seed case changes the noise realization while keeping its main amplitude 0.35.)

Both models select epoch 3. On the main noisy input the denoiser's MSE 0.05995 is about 3.6%
below the raw input's 0.06217, but the ordinary control is better at 0.05371. With low noise,
the raw input's MSE 0.01202 is far below either reconstruction's error. With no corruption,
the denoiser changes a perfect observation into an approximation with MSE 0.06496. Stronger
noise gives the denoiser an advantage over leaving input unchanged, but the ordinary control
still scores lower. The second independent draw produces similar, not identical, scores.
Two test noise seeds do not estimate variability across training seeds.
These are outcomes of a short fixed-budget experiment; they do not disprove the population
objective or show a general advantage for either training scheme.

The gallery retains those limits. Columns show the first held-out example of each digit 0–9,
without quality filtering. Rows are clean original, main corruption, denoised, ordinary control
on the same corruption, absolute denoising error, fitting mean, denoiser on clean input and
denoiser on strong corruption. Every panel uses grayscale [0,1]; errors are not amplified.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/denoising_reconstructions.png", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  std::map<std::string, n::Tensor> data, out;
  for (const auto& [name, value] : n::load_npz("build/denoising_data.npz"))
    data.emplace(name, value);
  for (const auto& [name, value] : n::load_npz("build/denoising_outputs.npz"))
    out.emplace(name, value);
  const auto                labels = data.at("test_labels").to_vector<int>();
  const auto                ids    = data.at("test_source_ids").to_vector<int>();
  std::vector<std::int64_t> rows;
  for (int digit = 0; digit < 10; ++digit) {
    const auto found = std::find(labels.begin(), labels.end(), digit);
    if (found == labels.end())
      return 1;
    rows.push_back(found - labels.begin());
  }
  const auto             clean    = data.at("test_images").reshape({200, 784});
  const auto             denoised = out.at("noisy_denoising_reconstruction");
  std::vector<n::Tensor> panels;
  for (const auto& values : std::vector<n::Tensor>{
           clean,
           data.at("test_corrupted").reshape({200, 784}),
           denoised,
           out.at("noisy_ordinary_reconstruction"),
           n::abs(denoised - clean),
           out.at("mean"),
           out.at("clean_denoising_reconstruction"),
           out.at("strong_denoising_reconstruction")})
    panels.push_back(n::take_rows(values, rows));
  n::save_image_grid(
      n::concat(panels, 0).reshape({80, 28, 28, 1}),
      "build/denoising_reconstructions.png",
      10,
      0,
      1,
      2,
      .8,
      3);
  std::print("| digit | official source ID |\n|---:|---:|\n");
  for (int digit = 0; digit < 10; ++digit)
    std::print("| {} | {} |\n", digit, ids[rows[digit]]);
}
```
