\caption(Eight matched rows expose blurred reconstructions, mild-noise damage and the ordinary control's performance. Integer pixel enlargement is three, with two-pixel gaps.)
\alttext(An eight-row gallery shows clean handwritten digits, noisy inputs, two models' reconstructions, absolute errors, a constant mean and two further denoiser input controls.)

The optional development oracle `scripts/check_denoising_autoencoder.py` reads the native
archives without preparing data or fitting. Its independent NumPy reference checks all
2,822,400 standard Gaussian values (maximum difference below 4.5e-16), all 3,136,000 corrupted
pixels bit for bit, both sets of 50,992 first-batch parameter derivatives (errors below 7e-11)
and both Float32 first Adam updates bit for bit. Across five test conditions and both models,
all 1,568,000 reconstruction pixels agree within 5.4e-7. Twelve parameter snapshots, both first
batches/gradients, the clean/noisy data, all three training corruptions and all original test
outputs also match an execution of the original C++ example exactly.

> Conclusion

The defining distinction is the target: corrupted inputs are scored against their clean originals.
A conditional-mean optimum explains why pixel MSE can favour smooth averages when observations
are ambiguous. Exact noise banks, visible optimizer updates and matched initialization/order make
this experiment reproducible, while independent validation selection keeps test cases for evaluation.
This small three-epoch denoiser offers only a modest main-noise improvement over leaving the input
unchanged; it loses to its ordinary control and damages lightly corrupted images. One noise family,
a small fitting pool and one training seed do not establish universal denoising, semantic code
quality, a generative image prior, classification accuracy or a compressed-file bit rate.

> References

- Vincent, P., Larochelle, H., Bengio, Y. and Manzagol, P.-A. (2008). Extracting and Composing Robust Features with Denoising Autoencoders.
  [ICML paper](https://www.cs.toronto.edu/~larocheh/publications/icml-2008-denoising-autoencoders.pdf),
  [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1145/1390156.1390294). The present example uses a ReLU bottleneck, untied weights,
  clipped Gaussian corruption and pixel MSE rather than reproducing the paper's masking-noise benchmark.
- Box, G. E. P. and Muller, M. E. (1958). A Note on the Generation of Random Normal Deviates.
  [Annals of Mathematical Statistics 29(2), 610–611](https://doi.org/10.1214/aoms/1177706645).
- LeCun, Y., Bottou, L., Bengio, Y. and Haffner, P. (1998). Gradient-based learning applied to document recognition.
  [Proceedings of the IEEE 86(11), 2278–2324](https://doi.org/10.1109/5.726791).
